{
  $schema: 'https://docs.renovatebot.com/renovate-schema.json',
  // helpers:pinGitHubActionDigests turns every `uses: owner/action@v7` into
  // `owner/action@<40-char sha> # v7.0.1`. A tag can be moved after the fact
  // (tj-actions/changed-files, March 2025: a retargeted tag dumped the secrets
  // of every workflow that ran it); a commit SHA cannot. scripts/pin-action-shas.sh
  // did the one-off migration and scripts/check-action-shas.sh fails CI on a new
  // unpinned reference. The preset only touches the github-actions manager:
  // Docker images keep the tags and digests the compose rules below manage.
  extends: ['config:recommended', 'helpers:pinGitHubActionDigests'],
  timezone: 'Europe/Amsterdam',

  // Deliberately NO top-level schedule. This is a self-hosted run: the cadence
  // is the workflow cron in .github/workflows/renovate.yml, and a second
  // schedule here only creates a window Renovate must ALSO be inside.
  //
  // That is exactly what went wrong between Feb and Aug 2026: the cron fired at
  // 05:00 UTC (07:00 Amsterdam) and GitHub delays scheduled runs by 1-3h, so
  // every one of the 28 runs landed between 08:00 and 10:30 Amsterdam — always
  // outside the old 'before 6am on monday' window. Renovate extracted
  // dependencies, found itself unscheduled, and finished without opening a
  // single PR. A silent no-op for 28 weeks, which is why transitive pins
  // (h2, idna, cryptography, pypdf) drifted until a manual audit caught them.

  // custom.regex has to be listed for the customManagers below to run at all.
  // It was missing, so the litellm-tests.yml and vexa-bot regex managers had
  // never run: #1732 moved the LiteLLM image and left the PyPI pin behind.
  enabledManagers: [
    'custom.regex',
    'docker-compose',
    'dockerfile',
    'github-actions',
    'npm',
    'pep621',
  ],

  // klai-hubspot is a sandbox-only POC (HubSpot Help Desk card, last real
  // commit 2026-05-27) that ships through HubSpot's own `hs project deploy`
  // lifecycle, not through this repo's CI. No workflow touches the path, so
  // its PRs can never get the required `quality` check and would sit open
  // forever — and merging them would not reach anything running anyway.
  // Re-enable the moment it gets a build/deploy job here.
  ignorePaths: ['klai-hubspot/**'],

  // Renovate merges through the API itself instead of arming GitHub's
  // auto-merge. Verified on PR #920 (2026-08-14): every check green, GitHub
  // auto-merge armed, and it still sat there — GitHub's auto-merge evaluates
  // the branch protection generically and reports REVIEW_REQUIRED, ignoring
  // the bypass allowance granted to the klai-renovate app. Renovate's own
  // merge call runs AS the app, so the bypass does apply.
  //
  // The cost is that merges happen on a Renovate run rather than the instant
  // checks turn green, which is why the cron below is hourly rather than
  // weekly.
  platformAutomerge: false,

  // Renovate's default prConcurrentLimit is 10, and on 2026-08-22 that default
  // deadlocked the whole queue. Majors were automerge: false back then, so six
  // of them sat open waiting for review; three BlockNote PRs were failing for a
  // config reason nobody had diagnosed yet; one eslint PR could never go green.
  // Ten unmergeable PRs, ten of ten slots, and a debug run saying it plainly:
  //
  //   Open PR Count: 10, Existing Branch Count: 10, Hourly PR Count: 0
  //   Calculated lowest branchConcurrentLimit among the upgrades present in
  //     this branch is 10.
  //   Reached branch limit - skipping branch creation
  //
  // Note the middle line: what blocks creation is branchConcurrentLimit, which
  // is null by default and inherits prConcurrentLimit. Setting the latter to 0
  // is therefore what lifts the former; there is no second knob to turn.
  //
  // Nothing new could be opened at all. Lock file maintenance — the thing added
  // specifically because transitive pins had rotted for 28 weeks — was among
  // the updates that could not get a branch. The failure mode is the same one
  // this file keeps rediscovering: Renovate runs, reports success, and silently
  // does nothing.
  //
  // The limit is the wrong tool here. It exists to stop a first run on a stale
  // repo from opening fifty PRs at once, but prHourlyLimit (default 2) already
  // paces that — at two per hour a backlog drains gradually no matter how large
  // it is. What prConcurrentLimit adds on top is a hard ceiling that anything
  // needing human review can permanently occupy, and reviewable-but-unreviewed
  // is the normal state of a major upgrade, not an exception.
  //
  // 0 means unlimited. Keep prHourlyLimit at its default; it is what actually
  // protects against a flood, and removing both at once WOULD be reckless.
  prConcurrentLimit: 0,

  // Mirrors min-release-age=3 in klai-portal/frontend/.npmrc and exclude-newer
  // in klai-connector. Three days, Dependabot's default since July 2026: the
  // hijacked npm releases of 2025 were gone after 2.5 h (chalk/debug) and 12 h
  // (Shai-Hulud 2.0), so 7 days bought nothing but a week of delay (2026-10-29). Without it
  // Renovate would propose a version the npm CLI then refuses to install while
  // generating the lockfile, producing a PR that cannot build. Keep the two
  // numbers in sync; the npm one also applies to every manual install.
  minimumReleaseAge: '3 days',
  // Since Renovate 42 an update WITHOUT a release timestamp counts as never
  // aged, and is held forever. GHCR, Quay, MCR and every other non-Docker-Hub
  // registry publish no timestamp, and neither does a lock file refresh. From
  // 2026-09-25 to 2026-10-22 that held the Docker images group (22 images,
  // among them Zitadel, Grafana, Caddy and Redis, behind five GHCR members),
  // LiteLLM and the lock file refresh on a `renovate/stability-days` check
  // that could never turn green. 'timestamp-optional' still ages everything
  // that HAS a timestamp (npm, PyPI, Docker Hub); only an update that can
  // never be aged goes through without the cooldown, which beats never.
  minimumReleaseAgeBehaviour: 'timestamp-optional',
  // A published vulnerability must not wait out the cooldown above: the 7 days
  // exist to let a hijacked release (litellm 1.82.7, March 2026) get pulled
  // before we take it, and a fix for a known CVE is the opposite case. Renovate
  // defaults to exactly this, so the keys restate the defaults on purpose: a
  // later "tidy-up" that sets minimumReleaseAge inside vulnerabilityAlerts, or
  // flips prCreation, fails scripts/test-renovate-config.sh instead of silently
  // holding a security fix for a week.
  //   - minimumReleaseAge: null  the vulnerability PR skips the cooldown.
  //   - prCreation: 'immediate'  no waiting for the stability-days check.
  // osvVulnerabilityAlerts adds the OSV database (npm, PyPI, GitHub Actions) next
  // to GitHub's own Dependabot alerts, which the klai-renovate app token may not
  // be allowed to read. It does NOT lift the other policies: a major still needs
  // the rules further down, a data store still waits on the dashboard, and the
  // version ceilings still apply.
  osvVulnerabilityAlerts: true,
  vulnerabilityAlerts: {
    enabled: true,
    minimumReleaseAge: null,
    prCreation: 'immediate',
    // The lock-file update of a security PR runs npm with THIS npmrc instead
    // of the repo's (npmrcMerge is false), so a fix younger than the cooldown
    // in klai-portal/frontend/.npmrc still resolves. The repo file keeps its
    // cooldown for people at a keyboard; legacy-peer-deps is copied from it.
    npmrc: 'legacy-peer-deps=true\nmin-release-age=0',
  },
  // Renovate otherwise installs the newest npm to regenerate lock files. npm
  // 12 defaults allow-remote to 'none' and refused the tarball of
  // @tailwindcss/oxide-wasm32-wasi, so every lock file refresh and thirteen
  // npm updates ended in EALLOWREMOTE (2026-10-21). CI and the Dockerfiles
  // run Node 24, which ships npm 11: generate locks with what installs them.
  constraints: {
    npm: '^11',
  },
  // With the default 'strict', a grouped update waits until EVERY member's
  // newest release is 7 days old. Ollama and LiteLLM release weekly, so the
  // Docker images group never aged: on 2026-09-25 Grafana 13.2.2 (out since
  // 2026-09-15) and Ollama 0.34 had been held back for weeks behind it, and
  // their Trivy findings with them. 'flexible' takes, per dependency, the
  // newest version that has already aged, so the group lands.
  internalChecksFilter: 'flexible',

  // The gap that caused the August 2026 audit findings. Renovate only bumps
  // DECLARED dependencies; vulnerable TRANSITIVE pins inside uv.lock and
  // package-lock.json are invisible to it. Lock file maintenance regenerates
  // those locks within the existing constraints, so transitive packages like
  // h2 and cryptography get refreshed instead of rotting.
  //
  // Supported for uv.lock via the pep621 manager and for package-lock.json via
  // npm (docs.renovatebot.com/modules/manager/pep621).
  //
  // Automerged, deliberately. A lock refresh has the widest blast radius of
  // anything Renovate does, which argues for review — but requiring review is
  // exactly what let the locks rot for 28 weeks in the first place, and the
  // realistic failure mode is nobody looking, not somebody looking and
  // catching something. Every service now gates on lint + pip-audit + its own
  // test suite, a failed deploy opens a ci-failure issue, and the refresh
  // lands as one squash commit that `git revert` undoes cleanly.
  // 'at any time' on purpose, and it is NOT redundant: lockFileMaintenance
  // defaults to its own 'before 4am on monday' window, which would walk
  // straight back into the bug described above — the cron fires at 03:00 UTC
  // (05:00 Amsterdam) and GitHub's 1-3h delay pushes the actual run past 04:00.
  // The workflow cron stays the only scheduler.
  lockFileMaintenance: {
    enabled: true,
    schedule: ['at any time'],
    automerge: true,
    rebaseWhen: 'conflicted',
    commitMessageAction: 'Refresh lock files',
  },

  // Renovate's docker-compose manager only reads `image:` keys. The Vexa bot
  // is pinned in an ENV value instead — `BROWSER_IMAGE: vexaai/vexa-bot:<v>` on
  // vexa12-runtime — because the runtime spawns bot containers itself rather
  // than Compose starting them. So Renovate moved the three vexa `image:` refs
  // and silently left the bot behind, which is how PR #1240 came to propose a
  // 0.12.23 runtime driving a 0.12.22 bot. Vexa is pre-1.0 and publishes no
  // changelog, so a patch-level protocol change between the two is not
  // something a reviewer can rule out.
  //
  // This makes the pin visible so the whole set moves as one PR. The pull the
  // pin needs is part of the compose sync: deploy/docker-compose.yml's bot
  // image must be on core-01 BEFORE the first spawn, because docker-socket-proxy
  // has IMAGES disabled (SPEC-SEC-024) and `compose up` never fetches an
  // env-valued image, so deploy-compose.yml pulls it from the commit being
  // deployed before it replaces any file (deploy/scripts/vexa-bot-prepull.sh).
  customManagers: [
    {
      // litellm-tests.yml runs the hook tests against the litellm PyPI
      // package at the version the proxy image runs, and
      // check-version-pin-agreement.py fails when the two differ. Without
      // this manager a LiteLLM image bump could never go green on its own.
      customType: 'regex',
      managerFilePatterns: ['/^\\.github/workflows/litellm-tests\\.yml$/'],
      matchStrings: ['litellm==(?<currentValue>[0-9][0-9.]*)'],
      depNameTemplate: 'litellm',
      datasourceTemplate: 'pypi',
    },
    {
      // The proxy image is versioned from the same PyPI release, not looked up
      // on GHCR. GHCR publishes no release timestamps, so the image would skip
      // the 7-day cooldown while the PyPI pin above waits it out: the two could
      // never agree (#1732: image 1.106.2, PyPI pin 1.96.2, check-version-pin-
      // agreement.py red for four weeks). One PyPI dependency moves both, and
      // keeps the cooldown on LiteLLM, whose PyPI release was hijacked in
      // March 2026 (1.82.7/1.82.8). Every PyPI release has a matching vX.Y.Z
      // tag on GHCR (checked 1.104.5 through 1.106.2); a release without one
      // fails the core-01 deploy on the image pull, loudly.
      customType: 'regex',
      managerFilePatterns: [
        '/^deploy/docker-compose\\.yml$/',
        '/^docker-compose\\.dev\\.yml$/',
      ],
      matchStrings: ['ghcr\\.io/berriai/litellm:v(?<currentValue>[0-9][0-9.]*)'],
      depNameTemplate: 'litellm',
      datasourceTemplate: 'pypi',
    },
    {
      // The visual tests run in the Playwright image, whose browsers must be
      // the build the installed @playwright/test expects. Looked up on MCR,
      // which publishes no release timestamps, the image skipped the 7-day
      // cooldown that npm waits out, so the two arrived as separate PRs that
      // could each only fail: #1876 (npm 1.64.1, image 1.63.0, "Executable
      // doesn't exist") and #1822 (image 1.64.1 alone). Reading the tag as the
      // npm package puts both in the Playwright monorepo group, one PR.
      // @playwright/test is pinned exactly in package.json for the same reason:
      // under ^1.63.0 the lock file refresh (#1749) moved it to 1.64.1 on its
      // own and broke the visual job the same way.
      customType: 'regex',
      managerFilePatterns: [
        '/^\\.github/workflows/portal-frontend\\.yml$/',
        '/^klai-portal/frontend/package\\.json$/',
      ],
      matchStrings: ['mcr\\.microsoft\\.com/playwright:v(?<currentValue>[0-9][0-9.]*)-noble'],
      depNameTemplate: '@playwright/test',
      datasourceTemplate: 'npm',
    },
    {
      // Qdrant pushes a Docker Hub tag before its GitHub release exists:
      // v1.20.0 was on Docker Hub on 2026-10-21 with no release or notes, and
      // the docker lookup proposed it (#1730). Qdrant holds the knowledge
      // vectors, so the version comes from the GitHub release instead, which
      // also gives the 7-day cooldown a publication date to count from. Every
      // release so far has a matching vX.Y.Z image tag; one without fails the
      // core-01 deploy on the image pull, loudly.
      customType: 'regex',
      managerFilePatterns: ['/^deploy/docker-compose\\.yml$/'],
      matchStrings: ['qdrant/qdrant:v(?<currentValue>[0-9][0-9.]*)'],
      depNameTemplate: 'qdrant/qdrant',
      datasourceTemplate: 'github-releases',
      extractVersionTemplate: '^v(?<version>[0-9]+\\.[0-9]+\\.[0-9]+)$',
    },
    // Docker Hub lists at most 1000 tags anonymously (page 11 answers 403),
    // and Renovate then drops EVERY Hub timestamp for that image. Under
    // timestamp-optional an update without a timestamp proceeds, so for the
    // images below the 7-day release age was never counted: meilisearch
    // v1.56.0 passed renovate/stability-days 43 minutes after its push
    // (#1730, debug run 40747506827 on 2026-10-28). Each of these reads its
    // version from GitHub instead, where releases and tags carry a date. The
    // depName stays the Docker Hub name so the data-store and major rules
    // below keep matching; packageName is the GitHub repository. A release
    // whose image is not on Docker Hub yet fails the core-01 deploy on the
    // pull, loudly, which has not happened inside a 7-day window so far.
    // postgres, python, node and nvidia/cuda hit the same cap and stay
    // unaged: their GitHub tags (REL_18_6, v3.12.x) cannot be mapped onto
    // the image tag by extractVersionTemplate, and endoflife.date carries
    // the cycle's first release date, not the patch's.
    {
      customType: 'regex',
      managerFilePatterns: ['/^deploy/docker-compose\\.yml$/', '/^docker-compose\\.dev\\.yml$/'],
      matchStrings: ['getmeili/meilisearch:v(?<currentValue>[0-9][0-9.]*)'],
      depNameTemplate: 'getmeili/meilisearch',
      packageNameTemplate: 'meilisearch/meilisearch',
      datasourceTemplate: 'github-releases',
      extractVersionTemplate: '^v(?<version>[0-9]+\\.[0-9]+\\.[0-9]+)$',
    },
    {
      customType: 'regex',
      managerFilePatterns: ['/^deploy/docker-compose\\.yml$/', '/^docker-compose\\.dev\\.yml$/'],
      matchStrings: ['image: mongo:(?<currentValue>[0-9][0-9.]*)\\n'],
      depNameTemplate: 'mongo',
      packageNameTemplate: 'mongodb/mongo',
      datasourceTemplate: 'github-tags',
      extractVersionTemplate: '^r(?<version>[0-9]+\\.[0-9]+\\.[0-9]+)$',
    },
    {
      customType: 'regex',
      managerFilePatterns: ['/^deploy/docker-compose\\.yml$/', '/^docker-compose\\.dev\\.yml$/'],
      matchStrings: ['image: redis:(?<currentValue>[0-9][0-9.]*)-alpine'],
      depNameTemplate: 'redis',
      packageNameTemplate: 'redis/redis',
      datasourceTemplate: 'github-releases',
      extractVersionTemplate: '^(?<version>[0-9]+\\.[0-9]+\\.[0-9]+)$',
    },
    {
      customType: 'regex',
      managerFilePatterns: ['/^deploy/docker-compose\\.yml$/'],
      matchStrings: ['image: rabbitmq:(?<currentValue>[0-9][0-9.]*)-alpine'],
      depNameTemplate: 'rabbitmq',
      packageNameTemplate: 'rabbitmq/rabbitmq-server',
      datasourceTemplate: 'github-releases',
      extractVersionTemplate: '^v(?<version>[0-9]+\\.[0-9]+\\.[0-9]+)$',
    },
    {
      customType: 'regex',
      managerFilePatterns: ['/^deploy/docker-compose\\.yml$/', '/^docker-compose\\.dev\\.yml$/'],
      matchStrings: ['pgvector/pgvector:(?<currentValue>[0-9][0-9.]*)-pg18'],
      depNameTemplate: 'pgvector/pgvector',
      datasourceTemplate: 'github-tags',
      extractVersionTemplate: '^v(?<version>[0-9]+\\.[0-9]+\\.[0-9]+)$',
    },
    {
      customType: 'regex',
      managerFilePatterns: ['/^deploy/docker-compose\\.yml$/'],
      matchStrings: ['grafana/grafana:(?<currentValue>[0-9][0-9.]*)'],
      depNameTemplate: 'grafana/grafana',
      datasourceTemplate: 'github-releases',
      extractVersionTemplate: '^v(?<version>[0-9]+\\.[0-9]+\\.[0-9]+)$',
    },
    {
      customType: 'regex',
      managerFilePatterns: ['/^deploy/docker-compose\\.yml$/'],
      matchStrings: ['ollama/ollama:(?<currentValue>[0-9][0-9.]*)'],
      depNameTemplate: 'ollama/ollama',
      datasourceTemplate: 'github-releases',
      extractVersionTemplate: '^v(?<version>[0-9]+\\.[0-9]+\\.[0-9]+)$',
    },
    {
      customType: 'regex',
      managerFilePatterns: ['/^deploy/docker-compose\\.yml$/'],
      matchStrings: [
        'BROWSER_IMAGE:\\s+(?<depName>vexaai/vexa-bot):(?<currentValue>\\S+)',
      ],
      datasourceTemplate: 'docker',
    },
  ],

  packageRules: [
    {
      description:
        'npm waits for its own 3 days (min-release-age=3 in .npmrc) and refuses \
        a younger version while regenerating the lock file. With flexible, a \
        dependency whose every new release is still inside the window got a \
        branch at the newest pending version anyway, npm answered ETARGET, and \
        the Dependency Dashboard listed it as Errored (@tanstack/react-query \
        5.104.5 and driver.js 1.10.2 on 2026-10-22, both younger than 7 days). \
        strict opens the branch only once a version has aged, which is when \
        npm accepts it. flexible stays for the Docker groups it was set for.',
      matchDatasources: ['npm'],
      internalChecksFilter: 'strict',
    },
    {
      description:
        'Klai LibreChat is intentionally digest-only and has no latest tag. Its \
        digest is produced by librechat-image-build.yml and enforced by the \
        deploy digest guard, so Docker tag discovery is not applicable.',
      matchManagers: ['docker-compose'],
      matchDatasources: ['docker'],
      matchPackageNames: ['ghcr.io/getklai/librechat'],
      enabled: false,
    },
    {
      description:
        'Klai presidio-analyzer is digest-only, exactly like Klai LibreChat: \
        the image is Klai\'s own analyzer (Dutch/credential recognizer pack \
        layered on the digest-pinned data-privacy-stack base), built and \
        pushed by presidio-analyzer-image-build.yml, and the pin is enforced \
        by deploy/check-klai-presidio-digest.sh. There is no tag for Renovate \
        to discover, which is why every run logged "Could not determine new \
        digest for update (docker package ghcr.io/getklai/presidio-analyzer)" \
        against deploy/docker-compose.yml — a permanent WARN that trains \
        people to skim past Renovate warnings, which is how a real one gets \
        missed.',
      matchManagers: ['docker-compose'],
      matchDatasources: ['docker'],
      matchPackageNames: ['ghcr.io/getklai/presidio-analyzer'],
      enabled: false,
    },
    {
      description:
        'Klai crawl4ai is built and tagged directly on the deployment host; \
        there is no published GHCR package for Renovate to query.',
      matchManagers: ['docker-compose'],
      matchDatasources: ['docker'],
      matchPackageNames: ['ghcr.io/getklai/crawl4ai'],
      enabled: false,
    },
    {
      description:
        'klai/rollback-canary is a tag the weekly rollback rehearsal sets on \
        core-01 (SPEC-INFRA-AUTO-ROLLBACK-001); it exists in no registry, so \
        every run would log a failed lookup.',
      matchManagers: ['docker-compose'],
      matchDatasources: ['docker'],
      matchPackageNames: ['klai/rollback-canary'],
      enabled: false,
    },
    {
      description:
        'vexaai/transcription-service is not published anywhere: it is built \
        from Vexa-ai/vexa source on gpu-01 (tag <upstream>-local-<stamp>), so \
        every run logged a failed lookup. Its upgrade path is the manual \
        build in docs/runbooks/transcription-service-bump.md, not a PR.',
      matchManagers: ['docker-compose'],
      matchDatasources: ['docker'],
      matchPackageNames: ['vexaai/transcription-service'],
      enabled: false,
    },
    {
      description:
        'Aqua blocks organization GitHub App tokens with its IP allowlist. Use \
        the public Git transport so action digest updates remain discoverable.',
      matchManagers: ['github-actions'],
      matchPackageNames: ['aquasecurity/setup-trivy'],
      overrideDatasource: 'git-tags',
      overridePackageName: 'https://github.com/aquasecurity/setup-trivy.git',
    },
    {
      description:
        'Aqua blocks organization GitHub App tokens with its IP allowlist. Use \
        the public Git transport for the Trivy version passed to setup-trivy.',
      matchManagers: ['github-actions'],
      matchPackageNames: ['aquasecurity/trivy'],
      overrideDatasource: 'git-tags',
      overridePackageName: 'https://github.com/aquasecurity/trivy.git',
    },
    {
      description:
        'Auto-merge patch, minor and digest updates once CI is green. Digests \
        are in the list because a grouped PR automerges only when every \
        update in it may: the Docker images group carried two presidio digest \
        pins next to its minors and sat open for a week with green checks \
        (#1927, 2026-10-22 to 2026-10-29) because digest had no automerge rule.',
      matchUpdateTypes: ['patch', 'minor', 'digest', 'pinDigest'],
      automerge: true,
      automergeStrategy: 'squash',
      // main does not require branches to be current. Rebasing every remaining
      // green PR after one automerge invalidates all of their checks and limits
      // Renovate to one merge per run. Preserve passed CI unless Git reports an
      // actual conflict; conflicting branches are still regenerated and tested.
      rebaseWhen: 'conflicted',
    },
    {
      description:
        'Group Docker images, and let patch and minor automerge like every \
        other dependency. This group was automerge: false from the start and \
        that is what PR #1306 measured: green and unmerged from 1 September \
        to 23 September, with mongo 8.3.11, redis 8.10.2, grafana 13.2.2, \
        ollama 0.34.3 and litellm 1.102.1 all queued behind it, unable to get \
        a branch because the group already had one. It is the same failure \
        this file documents for lockFileMaintenance — the realistic failure \
        mode is nobody looking, not somebody looking and catching something. \
        Majors still stop at the major rule below.',
      matchManagers: ['docker-compose', 'dockerfile'],
      groupName: 'Docker images',
    },
    {
      description:
        'Vexa automerges like every other image through the global rules, because \
        a bump is now self-contained. The bot image is an ENV value, so `compose \
        up` never fetches it and docker-socket-proxy cannot either (IMAGES \
        disabled, SPEC-SEC-024); deploy-compose.yml therefore pulls the \
        BROWSER_IMAGE of the commit being deployed \
        (deploy/scripts/vexa-bot-prepull.sh) before it replaces any file on \
        core-01, and a tag that does not exist ends the sync red with the host \
        untouched. Vexa is still pre-1.0 and publishes no changelog, so Renovate \
        reads v0.12.26 -> v0.13.1 as a minor where upstream means breaking; the \
        meeting E2E and the compose rollback are what catch that, as for every \
        other image. No matchManagers: this must cover both the three compose \
        `image:` refs and the BROWSER_IMAGE pin from the custom manager above. \
        Its own groupName matters as much as the rule: left in the Docker \
        images group, one pending vexa bump would hold every automergeable \
        image behind it and rebuild the jam this config just removed.',
      matchPackageNames: ['vexaai/**'],
      groupName: 'Vexa bot pipeline',
    },
    {
      description:
        'Twenty CRM automerges one minor at a time. Every bump is a schema \
        migration (the entrypoint runs `upgrade` on start) and Twenty only \
        upgrades cleanly from the previous minor, so skipping one is what \
        broke the 24 September jump. Three things make that safe: one PR per \
        minor (below), check-twenty-hop.sh in Deploy-script tests, which keeps \
        every PR but the next hop red, and check-twenty-upgrade.sh in the \
        core-01 deploy, which fails the deploy when the upgrade log does not \
        show this version migrating without failures. Its own group so a \
        pending CRM bump never holds back images that can merge alone. \
        See SPEC-INFRA-COOLIFY-EXIT-001 T2.',
      matchPackageNames: ['twentycrm/twenty'],
      groupName: 'Twenty CRM',
      automerge: true,
      // One PR per minor (and per major) instead of one PR that jumps to the
      // newest release. Upstream tests N-1 -> N far more than N-20 -> N, and
      // the 24 September upgrade showed what a long jump costs: v2.42.4
      // declared it could start from 1.21 and could not, because its first
      // step reads a column that only a 1.22 migration creates. Merged oldest
      // first, each PR is a single hop: merging one changes the image line,
      // the next PR conflicts, and the rebase-on-conflict setting regenerates it
      // from the version that just landed.
      separateMultipleMinor: true,
      separateMultipleMajor: true,
    },
    {
      description:
        'Data-store series bumps (minors) travel in their own group so a \
        waiting one never holds back the rest, and they automerge since \
        2026-10-29 (AGENTS.md: automatic by default, proof instead of \
        approval): the compose sync snapshots every store before its image \
        changes, probes after, and restores within 30 minutes on a red gate \
        (deploy/scripts/datastore-rollback.sh, proven on umami-postgres on \
        2026-10-28). Patches stay in the Docker images group. The rule right \
        below makes the minors arrive one series at a time.',
      matchDatasources: ['docker', 'github-releases', 'github-tags'],
      matchUpdateTypes: ['minor'],
      matchPackageNames: [
        'mongo',
        'mongodb/mongo',
        'postgres',
        'redis',
        'redis/redis',
        'rabbitmq',
        'rabbitmq/rabbitmq-server',
        'pgvector/pgvector',
        'getmeili/meilisearch',
        'meilisearch/meilisearch',
        'qdrant/qdrant',
        'falkordb/falkordb',
      ],
      groupName: 'Data store series bumps',
      automerge: true,
    },
    {
      description:
        'One minor per PR for the data stores, like Twenty: Renovate refuses \
        separateMultipleMinor next to matchUpdateTypes, hence a rule of its \
        own. A store moves one series at a time, so a failing series rolls \
        back alone and the MongoDB FCV step (each release starts only on the \
        FCV of the release before it) can follow each merge.',
      matchDatasources: ['docker', 'github-releases', 'github-tags'],
      matchPackageNames: [
        'mongo',
        'mongodb/mongo',
        'postgres',
        'redis',
        'redis/redis',
        'rabbitmq',
        'rabbitmq/rabbitmq-server',
        'pgvector/pgvector',
        'getmeili/meilisearch',
        'meilisearch/meilisearch',
        'qdrant/qdrant',
        'falkordb/falkordb',
      ],
      separateMultipleMinor: true,
    },
    {
      description:
        'BlockNote must move as one unit. The three packages re-export each \
        other\'s types, so bumping one alone makes npm nest a SECOND copy of \
        @blocknote/core under @blocknote/react/node_modules. TypeScript then \
        sees two structurally identical but nominally distinct BlockNoteEditor \
        classes and `tsc -b` fails on BlockPageEditor.tsx with TS2322 \
        ("Property \'options\' is protected but type ... is not a class \
        derived from ..."). That is what killed PRs #1158, #1159 and #1160 at \
        0.54.0. Verified 2026-08-22: with all three moved to ^0.54.0 together, \
        npm creates no nested copy and `tsc -b --force` exits 0 with no code \
        change. Renovate has no monorepo preset for BlockNote, so the group \
        has to be declared here.',
      matchManagers: ['npm'],
      matchPackageNames: ['@blocknote/**'],
      groupName: 'BlockNote',
    },
    {
      description:
        'Majors automerge like everything else (2026-10-29, AGENTS.md: \
        automatic by default, proof instead of approval). What a pull request \
        can test fails there and never merges; what only a production run can \
        test is deployed through deploy-with-rollback.sh or the compose sync, \
        proven by probe, error watch, data counts and E2E, and rolled back on \
        red with an issue and a revert PR (S5). A deploy or token action whose \
        new major breaks only on main fails that workflow closed: nothing is \
        deployed, the CI failure notice opens the issue, the next merge \
        carries the fix. The ceilings below are the only holds left, and each \
        pins a reproduced breakage with what lifts it.',
      matchUpdateTypes: ['major'],
      automerge: true,
    },

    // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
    // Version ceilings for upgrades that are BLOCKED, not merely unreviewed.
    //
    // Each of these was attempted or analysed on 2026-08-14 and found to be
    // impossible right now for a concrete, external reason. Without a ceiling
    // Renovate re-proposes them every hour; the PR then either fails CI or
    // sits open forever, and the next person rediscovers the reason from
    // scratch. `allowedVersions` keeps patch/minor flowing inside the safe
    // range while never offering the blocked jump.
    //
    // Each entry names the blocker and the tracking issue. When the blocker
    // clears, delete the entry — do not raise the ceiling by hand, or the
    // upgrade lands without the verification the issue asks for.
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
    {
      description:
        'typescript: pinned below 7 — typescript-eslint refuses TS 7 outright \
        ("typescript-eslint does not support TS 7.0"); its peer range is \
        <6.1.0 even on 8.67.0. Upstream: typescript-eslint#10940 (open). \
        Note tsc -b and all 495 tests PASS under TS 7, so a typecheck-only \
        gate would wave it through — lint and build are what break. \
        Tracking: GetKlai/klai#942',
      matchManagers: ['npm'],
      matchPackageNames: ['typescript'],
      allowedVersions: '<7.0.0',
    },
    {
      description:
        'victoria-logs: pinned at v1.51.x — v1.52.0 moved to a distroless \
        base with no executables, which breaks the CMD-SHELL wget \
        healthcheck. Already rolled back same-day once (25f2a5d95). Nothing \
        waits on that healthcheck via depends_on, but it is the ONLY \
        liveness signal: every Grafana alert queries VictoriaLogs itself and \
        runs execErrState: OK, so they all fall silent together if it dies. \
        Needs an external probe first. Tracking: GetKlai/klai#946',
      matchDatasources: ['docker'],
      matchPackageNames: ['victoriametrics/victoria-logs'],
      allowedVersions: '<1.52.0',
    },
    {
      description:
        '@tanstack/react-table: pinned below 9 — v9 removes useReactTable and \
        createColumnHelper from the main entry and drops the \
        getCoreRowModel table option. This is a code migration across 3 admin \
        pages, not a version bump, and 2 of those 3 have no test coverage. \
        The /legacy shim exists but ships deprecated. Revisit once v9 has \
        settled and the pages have tests.',
      matchManagers: ['npm'],
      matchPackageNames: ['@tanstack/react-table'],
      allowedVersions: '<9.0.0',
    },
    {
      description:
        'mongo: pinned below 8.1 until the FCV step is automated (branch \
        feat/datastore-major-migration lifts this ceiling). A reproduced \
        breakage, not a policy: each MongoDB release only starts on the \
        featureCompatibilityVersion of the release before it (8.2 needs FCV \
        8.1, 8.3 needs 8.2), the FCV bump is a separate command after the \
        upgrade, and Community has no binary downgrade. We ran 8.2 on FCV 8.0 \
        until 2026-09-26 and went back to 8.0 with a mongodump/mongorestore \
        (deploy/VERSIONS.md). With separateMultipleMinor in the data-store \
        group and the automated FCV bump after each rollback window, the \
        minors then flow one at a time on their own.',
      matchDatasources: ['docker', 'github-tags'],
      matchPackageNames: ['mongo', 'mongodb/mongo'],
      allowedVersions: '<8.1',
    },
    {
      description:
        'python (docker base): pinned below 3.14 — spacy 3.8.14 in \
        klai-connector publishes wheels for cp312/cp313 only, so a 3.14 base \
        image cannot install the dependency tree at all. Lift when spacy \
        ships cp314 wheels.',
      matchDatasources: ['docker'],
      matchPackageNames: ['python'],
      allowedVersions: '<3.14',
    },
    {
      description:
        'Python base-image minors and majors are a runtime upgrade of every \
        service at once (3.11/3.12 -> 3.13 arrived inside the Docker images \
        group on 2026-09-25 and broke two image scans), so they travel in \
        their own group: one PR, every service rebuilt and deployed through \
        the rollback path, and a red scan or probe rolls that one PR back \
        instead of the whole Docker images group. Automerge since \
        2026-10-29; patches stay in the Docker images group.',
      matchDatasources: ['docker'],
      matchPackageNames: ['python'],
      matchUpdateTypes: ['minor', 'major'],
      groupName: 'Python base image',
      automerge: true,
    },
    {
      description:
        'The Playwright image tag comes from the npm-backed custom manager, so \
        the github-actions manager must not also propose an MCR tag for it.',
      matchManagers: ['github-actions'],
      matchPackageNames: ['mcr.microsoft.com/playwright'],
      enabled: false,
    },
    {
      description:
        'LiteLLM image and the litellm PyPI pin the hook tests run against \
        move together, so the tests exercise the version the proxy will run. \
        Its own group so a LiteLLM change never holds back the other images.',
      matchPackageNames: ['litellm'],
      groupName: 'LiteLLM',
    },
    {
      description:
        'The LiteLLM image tag comes from the PyPI-backed custom manager, so \
        the docker-compose manager must not also propose a GHCR tag for it.',
      matchManagers: ['docker-compose'],
      matchPackageNames: ['ghcr.io/berriai/litellm'],
      enabled: false,
    },
    {
      description:
        'The Qdrant image tag comes from the GitHub-releases custom manager, so \
        the docker-compose manager must not also propose a Docker Hub tag that \
        has no release yet.',
      matchManagers: ['docker-compose'],
      matchPackageNames: ['qdrant/qdrant'],
      enabled: false,
    },
    {
      description:
        'Qdrant is proposed only once its GitHub release is 7 days old. Under \
        the global flexible filter, a dependency whose every new version is \
        still inside the window gets the newest one anyway with only \
        renovate/stability-days pending, which is how #1730 offered v1.20.0 \
        the day it appeared. strict opens no branch until a version has aged; \
        a branch that already exists is left as it is, not updated.',
      matchDatasources: ['github-releases'],
      matchPackageNames: ['qdrant/qdrant'],
      internalChecksFilter: 'strict',
    },
    {
      description:
        'These images are read from GitHub by the custom managers above \
        (Docker Hub drops their timestamps past 1000 tags), so the compose \
        managers must not also propose an unaged Docker Hub tag.',
      matchManagers: ['docker-compose', 'dockerfile'],
      matchPackageNames: [
        'getmeili/meilisearch',
        'mongo',
        'redis',
        'rabbitmq',
        'pgvector/pgvector',
        'grafana/grafana',
        'ollama/ollama',
      ],
      enabled: false,
    },
    {
      description:
        'Same reason as Qdrant: strict holds a branch until the version has \
        aged 7 days instead of opening it with only stability-days pending.',
      matchDatasources: ['github-releases', 'github-tags'],
      matchPackageNames: [
        'meilisearch/meilisearch',
        'mongodb/mongo',
        'redis/redis',
        'rabbitmq/rabbitmq-server',
        'pgvector/pgvector',
        'grafana/grafana',
        'ollama/ollama',
      ],
      internalChecksFilter: 'strict',
    },
    {
      description:
        'eslint (klai-docs only): pinned below 10 — eslint-config-next pulls \
        in eslint-plugin-react, whose lib/util/version.js still calls \
        context.getFilename(). ESLint 10 removed that method, so `eslint .` \
        throws "contextOrFilename.getFilename is not a function" while loading \
        the very first React rule and quality-docs never gets as far as \
        linting. klai-portal/frontend is already on eslint 10 and is \
        unaffected — it does not use eslint-config-next, which is why this \
        rule is scoped by matchFileNames rather than applied repo-wide. \
        \
        Re-verified 2026-09-01 against the current chain, not inferred from \
        peer ranges: eslint 10.9.1 + eslint-config-next 16.3.4 reproduces the \
        same crash. eslint-plugin-react is still 7.37.5 (April 2025) and its \
        `next` dist-tag is a 2018 release candidate, so there is no \
        prerelease route either. \
        \
        Do not reach for a fork or a patch step to get around this: it is a \
        dev-only linter and the upstream fixes already exist as PRs. Lift the \
        ceiling — delete this entry, do not raise the number — once all four \
        hold: (1) eslint-plugin-react publishes a stable release carrying \
        upstream #4022 with an eslint-10 peer, (2) eslint-plugin-import \
        publishes its already-merged #3230, (3) eslint-plugin-jsx-a11y \
        declares ^10 support, and (4) a plain `npm ci` without forcing plus \
        `npm run lint` in klai-docs both pass. \
        Tracking: GetKlai/klai#1184',
      matchManagers: ['npm'],
      matchPackageNames: ['eslint'],
      matchFileNames: ['klai-docs/package.json'],
      allowedVersions: '<10.0.0',
    },
    {
      description:
        'langchain-community: pinned below 0.4.2 — ragas 0.4.3 imports \
        langchain_community.chat_models.vertexai at ragas/llms/base.py:12, \
        and 0.4.2 removed that submodule as part of the langchain-community \
        sunset. Widening the bound makes every `from ragas.llms import \
        llm_factory` raise ModuleNotFoundError, taking the whole RAG-eval \
        harness with it (proven by PR #1100, which failed exactly there). \
        Lift only when ragas stops importing the Vertex AI integration.',
      matchPackageNames: ['langchain-community'],
      allowedVersions: '<0.4.2',
    },

    // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
    // Exact versions rolled back on core-01 (SPEC-INFRA-AUTO-ROLLBACK-001 S5).
    //
    // When the compose sync rolls back a Renovate image bump, deploy-compose.yml
    // runs scripts/renovate-revert-after-rollback.py, which opens a PR that puts
    // the image line back and excludes that one version here, so main does not
    // redeploy it and Renovate does not propose it again. Each entry excludes
    // exact versions only; the next release still flows. A package that already
    // has a ceiling above is refused, because a second allowedVersions rule
    // replaces the first instead of adding to it.
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
    // End of the rolled-back versions: scripts/renovate-revert-after-rollback.py inserts above this line.
  ],
}
